## Install

```bash
pip install {{ dist }}
```

Or for local development:

```bash
pip install -e .
```

## Use

Inside an Aegis Stack project:

```bash
aegis add {{ name }}
```

That will:
- Append a `_plugins` entry to your project's `.copier-answers.yml`
- Render any templates this plugin ships under `src/{{ pkg }}/templates/{% raw %}{{ project_slug }}/{% endraw %}` into your project tree
- Regenerate aegis-stack shared files so the plugin's wiring (routers, deps,
  cards, etc.) takes effect

## Layout

```
{{ dist }}/
├── pyproject.toml
├── src/{{ pkg }}/
│   ├── __init__.py
│   ├── plugin.py                 ← exposes get_spec() (the entry point)
│   └── templates/
│       └── {% raw %}{{ project_slug }}/{% endraw %}      ← files this plugin drops into the target project
└── tests/
    └── test_plugin.py
```

## Get listed

Publish to PyPI and the plugin is listed at
[aegis-stack.io/plugins](https://aegis-stack.io/plugins) after the nightly
sweep; the markers it looks for are already set in `pyproject.toml`.

Not publishing is fine too. A plugin installed from a path or a git URL works
the same way, and gets listed in the directory's source-only section if the
GitHub repository carries the `aegis-stack-plugin` topic. How both listings
work, and same-day submission:
[docs.aegis-stack.io/plugins](https://docs.aegis-stack.io/plugins/).

